// ============================================================================
// Projections · DECORATORS  (pure column views — no joins, no closures)
//
// ts_decorator and ts_decorator_argument are declared in souffle/decls_base.dl and
// populated by the parser, and BEFORE THIS FILE NOTHING IN graph/typescript/engine
// READ EITHER. The factory call already has an edge (call_sites carries a
// DECORATOR_CALL for `@Controller('/orders')`), so the invocation was never the gap.
// What was dropped is the pair that carries the FRAMEWORK meaning: the decorator's
// NAME and its ARGUMENTS. `@Get(':id')` reached the solver as "a call to Get" and
// never as "this method answers a route".
//
// The relation names here are Java's and Python's, deliberately and exactly:
// annotation_on / annotation_arg / annotation_arg_type_ref. A Java annotation, a
// Python decorator and a TypeScript decorator are three syntaxes for one fact — a
// name and some arguments attached to a declaration — so a framework rule written
// over that shape ports between the three front ends without edit. That portability
// is the reason this is a projection and not a set of ts_-prefixed rules.
//
// WHAT A TYPESCRIPT DECORATOR IS NOT: unlike Python's, it does not rebind the
// decorated name. `@Injectable() class S {}` leaves `S` referring to the class, so
// there is no analogue of py_decorator.replacesTarget and no rule here needs to
// decline an edge because a decorator was applied. TypeScript's own semantics are
// carried instead by decoratorSystem (c13): LEGACY_EXPERIMENTAL decorators can
// replace the target via their return value, ES decorators cannot, and a rule that
// cares must read decorator_system rather than assume.
//
// ts_decorator / lib_ts_decorator cols (21):
//   0 decoratorName  1 kind  2 context  3 ownerHash  4 tsTypeLinkHash
//   5 typeParameterHash  6 parentDecoratorHash  7 depth  8 position
//   9 startLine  10 endLine  11 isMetaDecorator  12 argumentCount
//   13 decoratorSystem  14 decoratorSemantics  15 tsExpressionLinkHash
//   16 resolvedDecoratorMethodLinkHash  17 tsModuleLinkHash  18 startColumn
//   19 serviceVersionLinkHash  20 tsDecoratorUniqueHash
//
// ts_decorator_argument / lib_ts_decorator_argument cols (13):
//   0 argumentName  1 argumentValue  2 valueType  3 position
//   4 parentDecoratorHash  5 referencedTypeHash  6 nestedObjectHash
//   7 arrayIndex  8 startLine  9 endLine  10 tsExpressionLinkHash
//   11 serviceVersionLinkHash  12 tsDecoratorArgumentUniqueHash
// ============================================================================

// ── annotation_on(Prov, Name, Kind, Context, OwnerHash, DecoratorHash) ───────
// The SHARED shape. Kind is BARE | CALL (`@Injectable` vs `@Injectable()`), and the
// distinction matters to a framework rule: a bare decorator has no arguments, so a
// rule reading a route path must require CALL or it silently matches nothing.
// Context is CLASS_DECLARATION | METHOD_DECLARATION | PROPERTY_DECLARATION |
// PARAMETER_DECLARATION | ACCESSOR_DECLARATION.
//
// OwnerHash is polymorphic BY CONTEXT — a ts_type for CLASS_DECLARATION, a ts_method
// for METHOD_DECLARATION — which is why decorator_target below splits it. A rule that
// joins OwnerHash straight onto methods() drops every class decorator without saying so.
annotation_on("client", name, kind, ctx, owner, h) :- ts_decorator(name, kind, ctx, owner, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, h).
annotation_on("lib", name, kind, ctx, owner, h) :- lib_ts_decorator(name, kind, ctx, owner, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, h).

// ── decorator_target(Prov, TypeHash, MethodHash, DecoratorHash) ──────────────
// The decorated entity, split so a rule does not have to switch on Context.
// tsTypeLinkHash (c4) is the OWNING CLASS in both contexts: for a class decorator it
// equals ownerHash, and for a method decorator it is the class the method belongs to.
// That is what lets a controller's class-level path and its method-level route be
// joined without walking containment.
decorator_target("client", t, owner, h) :- ts_decorator(_, _, "METHOD_DECLARATION", owner, t, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, h).
decorator_target("lib", t, owner, h) :- lib_ts_decorator(_, _, "METHOD_DECLARATION", owner, t, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, h).
decorator_target("client", t, "", h) :- ts_decorator(_, _, ctx, _, t, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, h), ctx != "METHOD_DECLARATION".
decorator_target("lib", t, "", h) :- lib_ts_decorator(_, _, ctx, _, t, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, h), ctx != "METHOD_DECLARATION".

// ── decorator_system(Prov, System, DecoratorHash) ────────────────────────────
// LEGACY_EXPERIMENTAL (the `experimentalDecorators` flag, what Nest and Angular are
// built on) | ES (the standard proposal) | UNKNOWN. Kept because the two differ on
// whether a decorator's return value replaces the target.
decorator_system("client", sys, h) :- ts_decorator(_, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, sys, _, _, _, _, _, _, h).
decorator_system("lib", sys, h) :- lib_ts_decorator(_, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, sys, _, _, _, _, _, _, h).

// ── decorator_expr(Prov, DecoratorExprHash, DecoratorHash) ───────────────────
// The decorator expression node, which for a CALL-kind decorator is also the
// call_site carrying callKind=DECORATOR_CALL. This is the join back to the edge that
// already exists, so a framework rule can say "the route registration is THIS site".
decorator_expr("client", e, h) :- ts_decorator(_, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, e, _, _, _, _, h), e != "".
decorator_expr("lib", e, h) :- lib_ts_decorator(_, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, e, _, _, _, _, h), e != "".

// ── decorator_module(Prov, ModuleHash, DecoratorHash) ────────────────────────
decorator_module("client", m, h) :- ts_decorator(_, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, m, _, _, h), m != "".
decorator_module("lib", m, h) :- lib_ts_decorator(_, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, m, _, _, h), m != "".

// ── annotation_arg(Prov, Name, Value, ValueType, Position, IsKeyword, ParentDecoratorHash, ArgHash) ──
// Same 8-column shape as Python's. IsKeyword is the constant "false" and that is a
// FACT, not a placeholder: TypeScript has no keyword arguments, so every decorator
// argument is positional. Carrying the column keeps a ported rule's join intact.
//
// ValueType is the discriminator a route rule must read. argumentValue is the
// parser's general value column, so a STRING check is what separates the path in
// `@Get('/x')` from the identifier in `@Inject(TOKEN)`.
annotation_arg("client", n, v, vt, pos, "false", parent, h) :- ts_decorator_argument(n, v, vt, pos, parent, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, h).
annotation_arg("lib", n, v, vt, pos, "false", parent, h) :- lib_ts_decorator_argument(n, v, vt, pos, parent, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, h).

// ── annotation_arg_type_ref(Prov, ReferencedTypeHash, ArgHash) ───────────────
// A decorator argument naming a class — `@Module({ providers: [OrderService] })`,
// `@Inject(CONFIG)`. This is the relation dependency injection is built on: the
// provider is named in an argument, never called, and without this row the wiring
// between a module and the classes it provides is unreachable.
annotation_arg_type_ref("client", t, h) :- ts_decorator_argument(_, _, _, _, _, t, _, _, _, _, _, _, h), t != "".
annotation_arg_type_ref("lib", t, h) :- lib_ts_decorator_argument(_, _, _, _, _, t, _, _, _, _, _, _, h), t != "".
